	</div>
<p style=3D"text-align:center">	<img alt=3D"Converting HEIC photos to JPG f=
iles with XnConvert" class=3D"img-responsive" height=3D"394" loading=3D"laz=
y" src=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures/xnconvert-convert-hei=
c-to-jpg.webp" width=3D"700"></p><p>	Your phone saves photos as HEIC becaus=
e it halves the file size, and then half the software on your computer refu=
ses to open them.</p><p>	The permanent fix is to convert them to JPG once, =
in bulk, and stop fighting the format.</p><p>	This guide does that in XnCon=
vert, which chews through hundreds of files in one go and leaves your origi=
nals exactly where they are.</p><div class=3D"note">	<p>		<strong>Not sure =
you want to convert at all?</strong> If you would rather <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.free-codecs.com/guides/how_to_open_and_view_heif_images.htm">open HEIC=
 photos without converting them</a>, install <a href=3D"https://www.free-co=
decs.com/download/heif_image_extensions.htm">HEIF Image Extensions</a> inst=
ead and Windows will read them natively. Converting is better when you need=
 to email, upload or share the photos.	</p></div><h3 class=3D"subtitle_head=
line">	Step 1: Download and install XnConvert</h3><p>	<a href=3D"https://ww=
w.free-codecs.com/xnconvert_download.htm">Download XnConvert</a>, run the i=
nstaller, then launch it from your desktop or applications folder.</p><p>	I=
t is free for personal and educational use on Windows, Mac and Linux. Using=
 it inside a company needs a paid licence, which starts at 15 EUR.</p><p>	N=
othing else is required. Recent versions read HEIC and HEIF straight out of=
 the box, so there is no plugin or codec to hunt down first.</p><p style=3D=
"text-align:center">	<a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures=
/xnconvert-add-files.jpg" target=3D"_blank"><img alt=3D"XnConvert Input tab=
 with the Add files button" class=3D"img-responsive" height=3D"224" loading=
=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures/xnconvert-add-=
files.jpg" width=3D"650"></a></p><h3 class=3D"subtitle_headline">	Step 2: A=
dd your HEIC files</h3><p>	On the <span class=3D"inline-code">Input</span> =
tab, click <strong>Add files</strong> and pick your photos, or just drag th=
em from File Explorer or Finder into the window.</p><p>	Whole folders work =
too, using <strong>Add folder</strong>. There is no practical limit, so thr=
ow the entire camera roll at it.</p><p style=3D"text-align:center">	<a href=
=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures/xnconvert-output-folder.jpg=
" target=3D"_blank"><img alt=3D"XnConvert Output tab showing the destinatio=
n folder setting" class=3D"img-responsive" height=3D"144" loading=3D"lazy" =
src=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures/xnconvert-output-folder.=
jpg" width=3D"650"></a></p><h3 class=3D"subtitle_headline">	Step 3: Pick wh=
ere the converted files go</h3><p>	Switch to the <span class=3D"inline-code=
">Output</span> tab and choose a destination folder.</p><p>	Send them somew=
here new rather than back into the source folder. That way your HEIC origin=
als stay intact and you can delete the copies if something goes wrong.</p><=
p style=3D"text-align:center">	<a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guide=
s/pictures/xnconvert-output-select-format.jpg" target=3D"_blank"><img alt=
=3D"XnConvert output format dropdown with JPG selected" class=3D"img-respon=
sive" height=3D"226" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/gu=
ides/pictures/xnconvert-output-select-format.jpg" width=3D"650"></a></p><h3=
 class=3D"subtitle_headline">	Step 4: Choose JPG as the output format</h3><=
p>	On the same <span class=3D"inline-code">Output</span> tab, open the <str=
ong>Format</strong> dropdown and select <strong>JPG</strong>.</p><p>	JPG is=
 the safe answer, because everything opens it. Pick PNG if you need a lossl=
ess copy, or WebP if the photos are going on a website and you want them sm=
all.</p><div class=3D"important-box">	<p>		<strong>Check this before you co=
nvert.</strong> The dropdown holds both input and output formats, and HEIC =
sits in the list too. Selecting HEIC here converts your photos to the forma=
t you are trying to get away from.	</p></div><h3 class=3D"subtitle_headline=
">	Step 5: Adjust the quality slider (optional)</h3><p>	Click <strong>Setti=
ngs</strong> next to the format dropdown to set JPG quality.</p><p>	Somewhe=
re around 85 to 90 keeps the photos looking untouched at roughly a quarter =
of the size. Below 70 you will start to see blocky patches in skies and ski=
n tones.</p><p>	The <span class=3D"inline-code">Actions</span> tab is where=
 the other 80-odd operations live, if you want to resize, rotate or waterma=
rk everything while you are at it. You can skip it entirely.</p><p style=3D=
"text-align:center">	<a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures=
/xnconvert-convert-files.jpg" target=3D"_blank"><img alt=3D"The Convert but=
ton in XnConvert" class=3D"img-responsive" height=3D"78" loading=3D"lazy" s=
rc=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures/xnconvert-convert-files.j=
pg" width=3D"650"></a></p><h3 class=3D"subtitle_headline">	Step 6: Hit Conv=
ert</h3><p>	Click <strong>Convert</strong> and watch the counter. A few hun=
dred phone photos take a couple of minutes on a normal laptop.</p><p>	When =
it finishes, open your output folder. Every file should now be a JPG that o=
pens anywhere, with the originals untouched where you left them.</p><p styl=
e=3D"text-align:center">	<img alt=3D"Converted photos sitting in the output=
 folder after conversion" class=3D"img-responsive" height=3D"394" loading=
=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/pictures/xnconvert-outp=
ut-folder-results.webp" width=3D"700"></p><div class=3D"success-box">	<p>		=
<strong>Save the setup for next time.</strong> Use the preset box at the bo=
ttom of the window to store this exact configuration under a name like "HEI=
C to JPG". Next month it is two clicks instead of six steps.	</p></div><h3 =
class=3D"subtitle_headline">	If your HEIC files will not load</h3><p>	Almos=
t every failure here comes down to one of three things.</p><ul class=3D"cus=
tom-bulleted-list">	<li>		<strong>An old XnConvert build.</strong> HEIC sup=
port improved over several releases, so <a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.=
com/xnconvert_download.htm">download XnConvert</a> again and install the cu=
rrent version.	</li>	<li>		<strong>The file is a Live Photo pair.</strong> =
The still image converts fine; the short video half is a separate file and =
will not come along with it.	</li>	<li>		<strong>The photo is still in iClo=
ud.</strong> A placeholder file on your disk has nothing to convert, so dow=
nload the full-resolution copy first.	</li></ul><p>	If you only need one or=
 two files done right now, <a href=3D"https://convertico.com/heic-to-jpg/" =
target=3D"_blank">HEIC to JPG online</a> and <a href=3D"https://convertico.=
com/heif-to-jpg/" target=3D"_blank">HEIF to JPG online</a> will handle them=
 in a browser with nothing to install.</p><h3 class=3D"subtitle_headline">	=
When XnConvert is the wrong tool</h3><p>	The tell is simple: if you never w=
ant to see a converted copy, you do not want a converter.</p><p>	Readers wh=
o just want to double-click a photo and see it should <a href=3D"https://ww=
w.free-codecs.com/heif_image_extensions_download.htm">download HEIF Image E=
xtensions</a>, which makes <a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/download/=
microsoft_photos.htm">Windows Photos</a> and File Explorer read HEIC direct=
ly.</p><p>	If your files are iPhone videos rather than photos, you need <a =
href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/download/hevc_video_extension.htm">HEVC=
 Video Extension</a> or the free <a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/dow=
nload/hevc-video-extensions-from-device-manufacturer.htm">HEVC Video Extens=
ions from Device Manufacturer</a> instead, since HEVC is the video cousin o=
f HEIC.</p><p>	And for a converter with a narrower, friendlier front end, <=
a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/guides/the_ultimate_solution_to_conve=
rt_heic_and_hevc_files__imazing_converter.htm">iMazing HEIC Converter</a> d=
oes this one job and nothing else.</p><h3 class=3D"subtitle_headline">	Othe=
r formats XnConvert will save you from</h3><p>	The same six steps work for =
any of the 500-plus formats it reads, which is the real reason to keep it i=
nstalled.</p><p>	Camera RAW files, PSD layers, JPEG XL and AVIF all go in t=
he same window and come out as JPG. For RAW specifically, <a href=3D"https:=
//www.free-codecs.com/download/raw_image_extension.htm">Raw Image Extension=
</a> lets Windows preview them first, and <a href=3D"https://www.free-codec=
s.com/download/adobe_dng_converter.htm">Adobe DNG Converter</a> is better i=
f you are archiving rather than converting.</p><p>	Going the other way, <a =
href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/download/webp_codec.htm">WebP Codec</a>=
 teaches older Windows apps to open the WebP files you export. Browse the r=
est of the <a href=3D"https://www.free-codecs.com/software/graphics.htm">gr=
aphics tools</a> if you need something more specialised.</p><div class=3D"f=
